Conflict resolved. We should indeed flatten the data structure and remove the namespace matching entirely. Every parameter in a given policy package should be flattened.
Implementation guidance: Flatten the policy data shape and update all related policy code, tests, and documentation to match. In policies/data.json, remove the compliance_framework namespace and per-policy package nesting so each policy package reads its parameters from flattened data keys exposed directly for that package/use case, per the human reply. Update policies/elbv2_tls_policy_approved.rego so approved_ssl_policies is read from the flattened data location instead of data.compliance_framework.elbv2_tls_policy_approved.approved_ssl_policies. Update policies/elbv2_information_movement.rego so approved_listener_protocols and approved_listener_ports are read from the flattened data location instead of data.compliance_framework.elbv2_information_movement.*. Update policies/elbv2_listener_https_enforcement.rego so allowed_plaintext_listener_arns is read from the flattened data location instead of data.compliance_framework.elbv2_listener_https_enforcement.allowed_plaintext_listener_arns. Preserve existing behavior: TLS policy matching remains exact AWS SSL policy name allow-list lookup; information movement protocol/port allow-lists remain opt-in with empty arrays meaning unrestricted; HTTPS enforcement still evaluates internal and internet-facing listeners the same, skips GENEVE, and only allows plaintext when the listener ARN is present in the flattened allowed_plaintext_listener_arns data value. Update tests and README/examples to use the flattened data shape, then run opa fmt on policies and ensure opa check --strict policies and opa test policies still pass.

lisa-design:
- Policy data parameters are flattened rather than nested under compliance_framework or package-specific namespaces. Each policy package reads its configurable parameters from the flattened data interface exposed by the agent, with defaults stored in policies/data.json using that same flattened shape.
